<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Juan de Ulúa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Fort of San Juan de Ulúa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Juan de Ulúa is a fortress on an island off the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Built from the sixteenth century to guard the main harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> and the treasure fleets, it was the scene of a defeat of John Hawkins and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> in 1568. Spanish forces held it until 1825, four years after Mexican independence, and it later served as a prison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua.jpg" alt="San Juan de Ulúa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: August Dominus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tvr%C4%91ava_u_Veracruzu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
